tegrated  market and france is one of the main  countries that sends out posted workers  so i think it's difficult for us to  defend our posted workers who live and  work in other countries  if we are to be against posted workers  from other countries coming to our  country i think this is where  nationalism actually has nothing to say  and we need european cooperation we're  not at the end of work here we have to  work on social security contributions  and benefits and we are negotiating this  with the regulation of with the revision  of regulation  843 and indeed colleagues did point out  that there were issues here and i do  agree with your ambition we want to  ensure this goes further this was  blocked at the end of 2021 we want to  ensure that we have mobility of benefits  and contributions and we hope that we  will get  this through we will be working very  hard on that because we think it's a  vital  plank underpinning the structure of  reform  various members spoke  about the fact that we need to show  vigilance and thank you for having  recalled this and i say this with great  humility because  there was a pottier law in france  whereby we have a provision as of 2017  whereby we have a system of vigilance  and we would like europe to adopt a  similar system we would like the  commission to put forward a proposal  asap  we know work has been done so we are  awaiting a commission proposal in the  next week so that at european school we  can set down obligations for european  companies on their value chains  respecting human rights the environment  which will mean that i can come back to  another question raised and companies  will be submitted to reporting  requirements our desire is that this  commission proposal be provided asap and  that it can go to the different  institutions and be worked on them asap  we also want to make progress on other  subjects i don't want us to forget  non-financial reporting of companies and  investors too  whereby we can set genuine standards for  responsibility and due diligence trilog  will be starting this week and that is a  very important point making it possible  again to obtain  very specific concrete results and that  is totally in line with the point you  raised i also wanted to come back  clearly to  fighting uh fraud  you mentioned that point and the fact  that again we need to make progress too  on the work that wasn't in fact  concluded on minimum tax of  multinationals and we do hope  that we will be able to get all the  agreements necessary and to finalize  this work over the next few weeks and i  share the level of ambition raised here  then there was a question that was put  about schengen once again romania like  bulgaria and now croatia  are  want to join schengen preparatory work  has been done and that's been recognized  by all the member states and the  procedure is going hand in hand with the  reforms we'll be  launching in a few weeks which will make  it possible once again to guarantee the  protection of all the external borders  and on schengen i would say quite  clearly on the one hand we have a  proposal from the commission from  december whereby we can better defend  the internal borders which was the point  that was raised a while ago and we hope  to conclude on that subject  and i would like to reiterate the fact  that our desire is to make progress on  all of these points as i said earlier  in taking over  responsibility for the tools we have  available to us like frontex and the  borders agency and using  intergovernmental cooperation to beef up  the borders and in concluding as well  the system that we have for cooperation  on borders well that means that we can  more effectively  introduce returns for example to  countries of origin and i think we are  not sufficiently effective as things  stand on this point  and i think we will manage to introduce  reforms which will make us more  effective in europe i also had questions  put to me on russia  i think i answered earlier  i think our credibility  vis-a-vis russia  lies primarily in entering into  uh demanding dialogue and we see that  looking at the dialogue that the us and  russia are currently undertaking  i think that it is good for there to be  coordination between europe and the us  but it is vital that europe has its own  dialogue with russia  it's also true that our collective  security  arms  on our territories is a european issue  too  so we have to conclude a common position  on the new world order for europe for  security and stability as i mentioned  and then collectively two  we have to work on areas where we are  vulnerable as various of you have  mentioned because the credibility of our  response does require  a certain amount of codependence whereby  we can mutually reinforce cooperation  amongst ourselves  avoiding therefore such vulnerabilities  and on energy issues for example this is  vital we've been reactive about this in  the last few months but we have seen too  that this is something that can be used  by regimes hybrid attacks  we've seen migration movements being  manipulated we've seen cyber attacks  we've seen hikes in gas prices and on  this front we need to build in  collective resilience  together lucidly i'd also like to  reassure people about farmers and people  living in the rural world i don't think  at all that they have nothing to do with  the europe that we  are talking about on the contrary when  talking about our cap  our farmers on the ground know to what  extent they need europe and it's thanks  to this european policy that we can make  progress and we also know collectively  that as it's linked the is the  solidarity investment policy that we had  during the crisis has made it possible  for us to make progress so the policies  that we have the european policies that  permit these major  investments in infrastructure and  immobility europe is a major contributor  and i wouldn't forget that  various people have also mentioned  macroeconomic reforms  incidentally  you've referred to this with some  nuances some differences and i think  that reflects your political vantage  point of course  including france  and including also the fact that some of  you thought that this was a new policy a  great opening  a kind of reinvention of the categories  that we used to have in the past in  terms of expenditure and others who  argued that this was a matter of coming  back to budgetary rules that used to  prevail prevail in the past either all  now first of all as the presidency of  the council for this uh semester i don't  want to anticipate what we might have to  say on this issue the discussions that  are still to come but i would like to  clarify a couple of points if i may as  far as i know in our texts in our  instruments we've never had a myth or a  cult of zero deficit zero debt  also because in the rate environment  that we live in that would be  counter-cyclical actually also i would  like to reiterate that france before the  crisis reduced its public deficit  several of you mentioned this coming out  of the excessive deficit procedure 2017  and we really brought that down quite  significantly and also brought down  taxation whilst introducing reforms so  we talked about a magic potion earlier  on i wanted to pick up on that and i  wanted to tell you that in this period  of crisis we still have our  insurance policy against unemployment  nobody else had done that and therefore  i think that this is certainly a  philosophy that i will stand by i will  support it i don't want people to mock  france on the contrary this is a  question of of an effort a genuine  effort made by all french people all  french citizens  now the next question can we come out of  this crisis and can we come back to  status quo ante where we were before  well first of all collectively in this  crisis we actually had far fewer  divergences than we did last time around  last time we had a crisis in the  previous financial crisis which then  became a sovereign debt crisis we  actually saw europe being splintered it  split apart  this time we have a solidarity-based  investment system in practically all of  our countries were all agreed and these  policies are monitored assessed and for  those of you who are concerned about  france let me tell you that a nobel  peace nobel prize winner a few days ago  said that the french solution was not  amongst the the worst you could have not  at all  and then let me also tell you that we  have a policy we've all invested into  this in order to support our economies  and then europe has also invested with  own resources in order to move even  further forward and i think that that  was the right choice the right policy  because the other policy  when we had the financial crisis that  was the other policy that we conducted  and then we actually made the  adjustments domestically at national  level there was a fragmentation monetary  reply was slow to come and we had  different budgetary policies so what  happened then europe took  a lot longer than the united states of  america coming out of that crisis years  more even though we did not cause the  crisis we actually weakened our own  productive capacity and we actually  achieved a kind of  divergence of different  economic policies that's not the case  today and in fact france has come back  to its gdp figures and therefore  collectively speaking i would like to  stand by the policy that we have rolled  out now in terms of coming out of this  crisis i don't want to anticipate yet  again but i'd like us to look at this in  light of the strategies that we've all  depended defended sorry depending on our  our vantage point nevertheless we've  said we have to invest in in the fight  against climate change we all said we  need reforms we need uh bans you might  have disagreements as to the pacing of  this and as to how we should go about it  but we certainly have to invest  innovation our productive  industries we need to of course stand by  some territories particularly rural  areas  the middle classes and also the working  classes  we need to stand by them of course and  for all of that we need to make a common  investment and in order to get a more  high-tech europe a technological europe  we need to have investments national  investments and also european  investments and also stronger defense i  agree we should go further but for that  we need to put money on the table we  need to make investments and again  look at the different countries look at  greece for instance greece is  shouldering a lot of the european  expenditure here some of us are very far  from the two percent and yet it's all  about collective security that's what  we're working towards so we need to look  at the budgetary terms as well and so  therefore yes i am in favor of having  investments we need these investments  and we can have a common analysis what  are the investments that we could you  know take from the private sector and i  think i've mentioned that taxonomy by  the way is part and parcel of that and  then there's another part if we want to  go sufficiently quickly ahead if we want  to be there stand by people then we need  to have public investments as well state  investments so there's a mass of public  investment which we need if we want to  be ambitious on climate on military  aspects on social aspects on industrial  aspects we need to be there we need to  step up to the plate whether it's  community whether it's national that's  the debate that we're going to have and  we need to have the right instruments in  hand in order to reach that target  that's the debate and that is what will  conduct leaders sorry to a new  macroeconomic policy with the  investments priority investment on all  of these big areas that i've talked  about either at community level or at  domestic level  in a different way but one way or  another we'll need to come up with the  funding we'll need to come up with the  financing and that whether that's  community finding new funding sorry with  new owned resources in order to finance  this this these policies that's one  thing all domestically with national  funding  that's the debate that we're going to  have we know that this generation is  really facing a turning point from that  point of view i was also asked about  another issue  namely regional languages and  multilingualism  and  you know that the minister of foreign  affairs of france is a a defender a  champion of these rights linguistic  rights first of all we very much hold by  these principles of multilingualism in  the european institutions i'm going to  say this here and here and now in front  of the european parliament we're in  defense of the french language of course  and in addition to that  we must not forget you know what it was  that lay at the very foundations of  europe post brexit etc you are if you  like the embodiment of that multiplicity  also regional aspects have their have  their room in all this have their place  in all this i'm not sure how you  interpret the legal decisions that you  you talked about earlier i don't want to  interfere in domestic legal proceedings  in spain but we certainly believe that  regional languages shall be respected  you know that  in france of course french is the only  official language but through education  through a proactive policy that we  should continue to pursue i think we  also want to make sure that regional  identities can be expressed can can be  lived a lived experience i'm also in  favor of being ambitious when it comes  to the south american continent several  of you uh mentioned this quite rightly  so i forgot to to mention it myself in  may yes indeed we intend to hold  together with our colleagues in spain  and in portugal we want to hold an  informal summit on the south american  continent and we want to talk about our  partnerships on that occasion so thank  you very much for recording the  importance of that event i  look forward to it and then the final  few points if i may uh joint military  operations  you in a way you might have surprised me  here mr danjong in your speech  you know this area very well indeed and  at the beginning of my term of office i  was able to rely on your expertise in  order to draw up a strategy document  which helped us pave the way for a new  french law on this particular aspect so  you know the situation very well indeed  and you know that france is very  committed to this here and in europe  and let me reply to you therefore sir  yes we still have joint military  operations we do this together in the  mediterranean in the uh our arab gulf uh  persia and also of course the task force  in cuba  so for all of these operations we are  talking about joint military operations  and incidentally let me point out that  this was done upon at the behest of our  greek colleagues and and in cooperation  with them you know we've always worked  together with you i can tell you france  is very much  devoted to this concept together with  you so we launched these operations  together we're also  uh procuring collectively for our  equipment  you know in terms of the rule of law  i've said what i've had to say but on  defense i can also state quite clearly  what i what my opinion is you know we  don't have enough um  we don't have enough uh distance between  what's happened in the past and today i  would like to commend the initiatives  taken by germany france others  i would like to also talk about  the permanent structured corporation  that we now have which set it up some  years ago now we still have  projects  up and running the twista et cetera i  won't list them all we also have a  european defense fund which we have set  up together  with principles common principles we  fought for that and i agree i agree with  you we could go a lot further it's not  the end of the story and i'm relying on  your support i'd like us to go further  to make these rules even more robust to  make them genuinely european but we are  the main investors when it comes to  research and technology in europe in  that particular place and we know that  the commission is working with us  closely and we all know how important it  is also we have a common uh cultural  strategy with a european initiative for  intervention proposed at the salbon  10 countries or so have decided to sign  up to this so that's another example and  thanks to the work done by the high  representative we'll be able to bring  that particular  compass project to an end as well  to completion  and  these are a series of projects that i'm  listing here before you just to  illustrate my point you can indeed  cite such examples and like yourself i  believe in action rather than words and  i believe in fact i believe in realities  rather than grand declarations grant  statements finally a question was put to  me on the issue of china and on the  massacre of the oigo community  i turned to the honourable member and  you were quite right to talk about  deportation about forced labor and about  these attacks first of all i agree with  what you said uh you know the fact that  there are several brands uh that have  been affected by this crisis and we now  have a framework in france in order to  move forward on these issues in terms of  corporations  that you're quite right to mention that  secondly you know that france every time  is very clear bilaterally on these  issues and we were perfectly clear also  with chancellor merkel president michel  michelle president vander lyon  we raised this issue and we said that  this was a condition for the investment  agreement now why do i want to respond  to that particular point  in quite some detail you you talked  about your commitment and i wanted to  respond by saying this there's not going  to be an investment agreement if we  don't have ratification of the  conventions of the ilo that would allow  us to  monitor and to ban of course forced  labor that's point number one point  number two and this is something that  was mentioned by several of your  colleagues on due diligence this is  precisely the instrument that will allow  us to implement what you're calling for  so let me state quite clearly before you  i am in favor of that this proposal  should be submitted as soon as possible  and i very much hope that it will be  finalized as soon as possible that we  will complete it we support it in france  we continue to apply it in france we  would like it to become an eu regulation  and i think that that is the framework  that will allow us to impose this  obligation on our businesses our  corporations also protect those  corporations that are  subscribing to due diligence principles  and as for the commission the commission  will also come forward with its proposal  for a text or it might have an ad hoc  approach but to ban the import of goods  that are the result of forced labor a  ban on such imports that is what europe  can do so let's do it  ladies and gentlemen these would be my  replies to your  questions comments  as i picked up on certain points that i  wanted to make perhaps more specifically  and respond to you  i was very happy and very proud to be  able to stand before you here today in  strasbourg in this parliament whom we  love so dearly  as do you and i would like to thank you  very much indeed for defending the  parliament in strasbourg  first of all because this is the spirit  and the letter of our texts  furthermore because the europe that we  believe in is a europe that doesn't have  a single center it is actually a  polycentric europe  you have fillers pillars which are  present in different countries  and therefore  i believe that the presence of the  european parliament in strasbourg and  the progress that we've been able to  make and i would like to thank all of  the teams the secretary general and the  teams that have really helped us these  last few weeks and months you know that  france has really made an investment in  order to provide good working conditions  with new premises allowing you to work  better and to be welcomed even better  here in strasbourg it's because we  believe in this and we believe that this  is the europe that we want to defend the  values that we want to defend our  mirrored in this european parliament in  strasbourg so that is what i wanted to  say thank you i believe in europe of  ambitions and that is what we need we  need a unified europe we need a europe  that stands together and i know that you  all know notwithstanding differences and  opinion that we've seen today and that  we can have of course amongst each other  but we know that we can have a  respectful dialogue we can have  exchanges sometimes moot points  sometimes controversial points but  nevertheless we can come to good  compromises ones that are ambitious ones  that allow us to build europe and to  take europe into the future take it  further respecting all differences  respecting also the fact that we  sometimes agree to disagree but at the  same time we're always seeking new  agreements so thank you very much for  your kind attention honourable members  mercy  may see  [Applause]  thank you to you mr president into all  college  you

